Okay.

00:04:45.435 --> 00:04:46.268
I'm gonna get the
information on what that is.

00:04:46.268 --> 00:04:48.121
Okay, so congrats on the new book.

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:49.561
And so the new book is called,

00:04:49.561 --> 00:04:50.862
"Have I Told You This Already?"

00:04:50.862 --> 00:04:51.870
So why the title?

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:53.401
Do you tell a lot of
stories over and over again?

00:04:53.401 --> 00:04:54.234
- Yes.

00:04:54.234 --> 00:04:55.628
That's one of the main reasons.

00:04:55.628 --> 00:04:59.081
I guess it's sort of a nod to the age I am

00:04:59.081 --> 00:05:01.931
which is an age where
I've had such good friends

00:05:01.931 --> 00:05:05.060
for so long that in some
cases I can't remember

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.081
if I've told them the story already,

00:05:07.081 --> 00:05:09.321
if they were there with
me when it happened.

00:05:09.321 --> 00:05:11.671
If they know the most recent version like

00:05:11.671 --> 00:05:13.803
it's sort of just about

00:05:13.803 --> 00:05:16.451
having known somebody a really long time

00:05:16.451 --> 00:05:19.073
and just, it came out of pandemic times

00:05:19.073 --> 00:05:21.241
of thinking of kind of the stories

00:05:21.241 --> 00:05:23.001
that have meant a lot to me in my life.

00:05:23.001 --> 00:05:25.241
Many of them told to me by my dad

00:05:25.241 --> 00:05:27.353
and sort of what's my version of that?

00:05:27.353 --> 00:05:29.291
Like what are my sort of
greatest hits I guess.

00:05:29.291 --> 00:05:30.124
- Yeah.

00:05:30.124 --> 00:05:31.451
Did you grow up wanting to be a writer?

00:05:31.451 --> 00:05:32.284
Were you always into it?

00:05:32.284 --> 00:05:35.262
- I grew up a really serious reader

00:05:35.262 --> 00:05:38.360
and that was kind of a
gift given to me by my dad,

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.593
who was a great storyteller of his stories

00:05:41.593 --> 00:05:42.426
from his own life,

00:05:42.426 --> 00:05:43.851
but also would read to me every night

00:05:43.851 --> 00:05:45.761
so I could read when I was really little.

00:05:45.761 --> 00:05:47.601
I actually skipped kindergarten

00:05:47.601 --> 00:05:50.081
because the teacher left
the room is the seventies

00:05:50.081 --> 00:05:51.721
and she came back.

00:05:51.721 --> 00:05:54.311
- Smoke break.
(both laughs happily)

00:05:54.311 --> 00:05:55.822
- And I was reading a book to the class

00:05:55.822 --> 00:05:58.971
and she wondered if I'd memorized it

00:05:58.971 --> 00:06:01.001
or if I actually could read.

00:06:01.001 --> 00:06:02.529
So reading was really important

00:06:02.529 --> 00:06:04.601
and storytelling I think is
how I ever got interested

00:06:04.601 --> 00:06:05.819
in being an actor.

00:06:05.819 --> 00:06:08.759
It's just like, I would imagine
that I was the panda bear

00:06:08.759 --> 00:06:09.592
or whatever.

00:06:09.592 --> 00:06:11.070
I don't know again what story this is,

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.531
but so writing kind of feels
like a natural extension

00:06:14.531 --> 00:06:15.531
of that I think

00:06:15.531 --> 00:06:16.379
- Yeah.

00:06:16.379 --> 00:06:19.038
- it's like a way for me to tell a story

00:06:19.038 --> 00:06:20.049
on my own you know,

00:06:20.049 --> 00:06:22.379
without needing a production necessarily.

00:06:22.379 --> 00:06:23.278
- Yeah and our imagination,

00:06:23.278 --> 00:06:24.111
I say it all the time,

00:06:24.111 --> 00:06:26.019
there's no budget with your imagination.

00:06:26.019 --> 00:06:27.038
So, it's like really cool.

00:06:27.038 --> 00:06:28.209
It's not like a movie or TV thing.

00:06:28.209 --> 00:06:29.486
You can really create whatever you want.

00:06:29.486 --> 00:06:31.138
That's why I love books.

00:06:31.138 --> 00:06:34.009
- I love fiction and this is non-fiction,

00:06:34.009 --> 00:06:39.009
but in this one in particular,
kind of the titles come to me

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:41.777
and then I write the story around it.

00:06:41.777 --> 00:06:42.610
- Yeah.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.567
- So it's been interesting
as I have more experience

00:06:44.567 --> 00:06:47.859
and as I write more, it's almost like

00:06:47.859 --> 00:06:49.657
I don't know if you have
this music, but it's more-

00:06:49.657 --> 00:06:51.070
- I was like, I do titles
like that by the way.

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:51.903
With songs.

00:06:51.903 --> 00:06:52.736
- Really?

00:06:52.736 --> 00:06:53.569
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:53.569 --> 00:06:54.499
I write, I literally
have one of my next album

00:06:54.499 --> 00:06:55.538
called Skip This Part.

00:06:55.538 --> 00:06:56.557
And I was like, I just
wanna skip this part

00:06:56.557 --> 00:06:58.149
of this whole devastating situation.

00:06:58.149 --> 00:07:00.637
And so I wrote the whole song
around, I do that as well.

00:07:00.637 --> 00:07:03.847
- So, I think it's like
that's how your brain

00:07:03.847 --> 00:07:04.978
tells a story, right?

00:07:04.978 --> 00:07:05.811
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:05.811 --> 00:07:06.967
- And so that's some of these

00:07:06.967 --> 00:07:09.898
there's one called Boobs of
the Nineties for example.

00:07:09.898 --> 00:07:11.997
(Kelly laughs happily)

00:07:11.997 --> 00:07:14.467
- That just kind of came to me

00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:15.869
and I was like, "What is this about?"

00:07:15.869 --> 00:07:16.749
And it's kind of about,

00:07:16.749 --> 00:07:20.647
it's about how things have
changed since the '90s

00:07:20.647 --> 00:07:24.018
but my boobs were stuck in the same place.

00:07:24.018 --> 00:07:25.707
I don't know, you read it,
you have to buy the book,

00:07:25.707 --> 00:07:26.989
(Kelly laughs happily)

00:07:26.989 --> 00:07:29.218
but in the way that I think probably,

00:07:29.218 --> 00:07:31.239
you know the song you think of the idea

00:07:31.239 --> 00:07:32.719
and then the rest of
the song comes to you.

00:07:32.719 --> 00:07:33.770
That's sort of what this is.

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:34.603
- [Kelly] Yeah because there's the theme.

00:07:34.603 --> 00:07:35.436
- Yeah.

00:07:35.436 --> 00:07:38.010
- [Kelly] Boobs and then '90s.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:38.961
- Old boobs.

00:07:38.961 --> 00:07:40.370
- [Kelly] So much.
(everyone laughs happily)

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:42.781
I wanna mention this quote from the book.

00:07:42.781 --> 00:07:45.477
It's "Maybe turning fears
about aging and mortality

00:07:45.477 --> 00:07:47.328
into contemplation and comedy

00:07:47.328 --> 00:07:49.959
is just one of those things
women are better at."

00:07:49.959 --> 00:07:51.310
So, what does that mean to you?

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:52.143
It's a great statement.

00:07:52.143 --> 00:07:52.976
- Thank you.

00:07:52.976 --> 00:07:54.226
I guess it was-

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:56.221
There's an essay where I'm talking about

00:07:56.221 --> 00:07:58.829
Nora Ephron specifically,

00:07:58.829 --> 00:08:02.119
who's a writer I really admire
who wrote, who was an essayist

00:08:02.119 --> 00:08:07.119
as to wrote a number of essays
on aging and being, you know,

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.258
noticing her friends all
wearing like turtlenecks,

00:08:10.258 --> 00:08:12.359
trying to hide that
they were getting older.

00:08:12.359 --> 00:08:14.479
And I just as a matter of research

00:08:14.479 --> 00:08:18.181
was looking into men
who've written about aging.

00:08:18.181 --> 00:08:21.681
And I looked up men, women's necks, aging.

00:08:22.789 --> 00:08:23.622
- Yeah.

00:08:23.622 --> 00:08:24.509
- The only thing that came up was

00:08:24.509 --> 00:08:27.501
why do are men so
attracted to women's necks?

00:08:27.501 --> 00:08:28.519
And I was like, what?

00:08:28.519 --> 00:08:30.869
Like there's no writing, there's no male.

00:08:30.869 --> 00:08:34.490
There is less men writing about aging

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:36.949
in any kind of comedic
way or contemplative way.

00:08:36.949 --> 00:08:38.969
And I thought, "Gosh, this
is maybe just something

00:08:38.969 --> 00:08:42.527
that women are really
trying to process and-"

00:08:42.527 --> 00:08:44.858
- [Kelly] And I think
it, people talk about it

00:08:44.858 --> 00:08:45.778
more with women.

00:08:45.778 --> 00:08:46.611
- Sure.

00:08:46.611 --> 00:08:48.130
- [Kelly] They're always
like, "Oh, he is a fine wine

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:48.963
- Yes.

00:08:48.963 --> 00:08:49.810
- [Kelly] and got better as he aged?"

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:50.643
- Yes.

00:08:50.643 --> 00:08:52.650
- Nobody says that about women.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.378
She looks so great at 60.

00:08:54.378 --> 00:08:56.229
You know, I'm saying nobody's
really generally saying

00:08:56.229 --> 00:08:59.090
that when they do, I find age beautiful

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:02.130
because I have had friends
that didn't make it past 30.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:02.963
- Yeah.

00:09:02.963 --> 00:09:03.796
- Like, you know what I'm saying?

00:09:03.796 --> 00:09:04.629
We're lucky.

00:09:04.629 --> 00:09:05.462
- Yeah.

00:09:05.462 --> 00:09:06.295
- I find these wrinkles.

00:09:06.295 --> 00:09:07.128
I earned these, I got these.

00:09:07.128 --> 00:09:07.961
- I agree.

00:09:07.961 --> 00:09:08.794
Yeah.

00:09:08.794 --> 00:09:09.627
- And it's a beautiful thing

00:09:09.627 --> 00:09:10.656
because not everybody's given that chance

00:09:10.656 --> 00:09:11.668
to have that lifetime.
(everyone claps genuinely)

00:09:11.668 --> 00:09:12.799
- Yeah.

00:09:12.799 --> 00:09:13.843
- [Kelly] And age is awesome.

00:09:13.843 --> 00:09:14.909
- Age is awesome.

00:09:14.909 --> 00:09:15.742
- It's interesting though

00:09:15.742 --> 00:09:17.458
that women are and maybe
we're more comfortable

00:09:17.458 --> 00:09:18.967
talking about it because
people project it on us

00:09:18.967 --> 00:09:20.037
all the time that-

00:09:20.037 --> 00:09:22.717
- I think it's like one of
those things women are good at

00:09:22.717 --> 00:09:26.766
which is turning a potential
negative into something

00:09:26.766 --> 00:09:29.615
that we can learn from or
something to feel that's funny.

00:09:29.615 --> 00:09:31.418
We can share, we can feel positive about,

00:09:31.418 --> 00:09:33.218
we can laugh with our friends.

00:09:33.218 --> 00:09:35.466
I just think it's kind of
a uniquely female thing

00:09:35.466 --> 00:09:38.716
at least in my limited Google
research of looking up women.

00:09:38.716 --> 00:09:39.549
- I know.

00:09:39.549 --> 00:09:41.138
I was thinking like, I
always think if I die,

00:09:41.138 --> 00:09:43.264
what are they gonna find in my history?

00:09:43.264 --> 00:09:44.097
- Yeah.

00:09:44.097 --> 00:09:44.930
- And I love that

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:48.266
like boobs of the nineties,
like women's necks.

00:09:48.266 --> 00:09:49.167
(Kelly laughs genuinely)

00:09:49.167 --> 00:09:51.677
It's like all your your
Google history is awesome.

00:09:51.677 --> 00:09:52.647
- Yeah.

00:09:52.647 --> 00:09:54.157
- So what, you've written a few books now,

00:09:54.157 --> 00:09:55.146
we were saying this is your fourth.

00:09:55.146 --> 00:09:56.047
So have you ever considered,

00:09:56.047 --> 00:09:57.146
I know you like science fiction.

00:09:57.146 --> 00:09:58.698
Have you ever considered doing that?

00:09:58.698 --> 00:09:59.948
- I don't know.

00:10:01.316 --> 00:10:04.327
I write very reality-based
but I think it would be cool

00:10:04.327 --> 00:10:07.774
to take one of my characters into space

00:10:07.774 --> 00:10:09.665
and see what would happen?

00:10:09.665 --> 00:10:11.076
- I think you would be great at it.

00:10:11.076 --> 00:10:12.276
You're, I mean you're a
great writer in general.

00:10:12.276 --> 00:10:13.173
- I mean, Hollywood is
kind of outer space.

00:10:13.173 --> 00:10:14.006
- It is outer space.

00:10:14.006 --> 00:10:14.839
- What do you think?

00:10:14.839 --> 00:10:16.196
- I would say it's the
capital from "Hunger Games."

00:10:16.196 --> 00:10:17.247
- Yes.

00:10:17.247 --> 00:10:18.295
- Where I'm from.

00:10:18.295 --> 00:10:19.305
Everyone looks real different here.

00:10:19.305 --> 00:10:20.476
- Yeah.
(everyone laughs happily)

00:10:20.476 --> 00:10:24.036
- So, well, since we both like
space and Thanksgiving food.

00:10:24.036 --> 00:10:25.727
Wanna play a game that we have a game

00:10:25.727 --> 00:10:28.217
called Planet or Pastry?

00:10:28.217 --> 00:10:29.447
- Okay, great.

00:10:29.447 --> 00:10:30.280
- [Kelly] Both are cool.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:31.113
- I know you're excited about it.

00:10:31.113 --> 00:10:31.946
I can feel it.

00:10:31.946 --> 00:10:32.967
We need a, you don't have a choice.

00:10:32.967 --> 00:10:34.606
You're playing Lauren.
- Okay, great.

